1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,800
[Merc sings an A, 220 Hz]

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:05,050
[Tavi sings an A, 440 Hz]

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,450
MERC: How much faster
does the higher A shake?

4
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:10,550
[think]

5
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:14,404
Twice as fast: an octave, one turn up.

6
00:00:14,904 --> 00:00:17,673
TAVI: Is there a hidden note
between every two letters?

7
00:00:18,023 --> 00:00:20,557
MERC: Seven letters, but twelve notes.

8
00:00:20,857 --> 00:00:23,107
TAVI: Then where do
the other five come from?

9
00:00:23,316 --> 00:00:24,846
MERC: Let's go and find them.

10
00:00:25,246 --> 00:00:28,446
[chime]

11
00:00:28,646 --> 00:00:33,653
By the end, you'll find all twelve,
hear the smallest step between them,

12
00:00:33,736 --> 00:00:34,785
and sing it.

13
00:00:35,885 --> 00:00:39,842
Listen: an A, then the very next note up.

14
00:00:40,192 --> 00:00:42,192
[A, then the next note up]

15
00:00:42,592 --> 00:00:47,753
It sits in the gap between A and B.
One of the five.

16
00:00:48,253 --> 00:00:52,471
TAVI: If we keep taking steps that small,
how many until we're back at A?

17
00:00:52,771 --> 00:00:54,271
MERC: Count the steps with me.

18
00:00:54,613 --> 00:01:00,613
[twelve steps up, from A3 to A4]

19
00:01:01,113 --> 00:01:04,968
Twelve steps,
and we're back at A, one turn up.

20
00:01:05,268 --> 00:01:08,977
220 Hz has doubled to 440.

21
00:01:09,377 --> 00:01:14,769
Seven letters, five notes between them:
twelve notes in every octave.

22
00:01:15,369 --> 00:01:20,627
TAVI: Last time, the gaps in hertz
kept growing. Do these steps grow too?

23
00:01:20,977 --> 00:01:27,787
MERC: In hertz, yes. To your ear, no: each
one turns the spiral by the same amount.

24
00:01:28,287 --> 00:01:31,611
That step has a name. A semitone.

25
00:01:32,011 --> 00:01:34,624
Some people call it a half step.

26
00:01:37,524 --> 00:01:40,346
Now unroll the spiral, like last time.

27
00:01:41,346 --> 00:01:47,736
Every bead lands on a key. Seven white
and five black, octave after octave.

28
00:01:48,236 --> 00:01:52,349
Look at B and C.
There's no black key between them.

29
00:01:52,849 --> 00:01:58,330
TAVI: But B to C looks just as far
as C to D. Isn't it the same step?

30
00:01:58,830 --> 00:02:05,517
MERC: Before you listen, predict.
Which step is bigger: B to C, or C to D?

31
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:07,263
Or are they the same?

32
00:02:07,563 --> 00:02:10,563
[pause and predict]

33
00:02:10,763 --> 00:02:14,163
[B to C, then C to D]

34
00:02:14,663 --> 00:02:21,313
C to D is the bigger one.
B to C is the smallest step a piano has.

35
00:02:21,713 --> 00:02:26,157
Listen to C and the black key above it,
then B and C.

36
00:02:26,507 --> 00:02:29,907
[C to the black key above it, then B to C]

37
00:02:30,307 --> 00:02:32,534
The same step.

38
00:02:32,617 --> 00:02:38,793
B and C are already neighbours, a semitone
apart, so no key fits between them.

39
00:02:39,193 --> 00:02:40,909
E and F, too.

40
00:02:41,159 --> 00:02:42,659
[E to F]

41
00:02:43,159 --> 00:02:46,726
Now draw every key the same width.

42
00:02:46,809 --> 00:02:51,851
Neighbours, white or black,
are always one semitone apart.

43
00:02:52,451 --> 00:02:56,151
[C, C sharp, D; then C straight to D]

44
00:02:56,501 --> 00:03:02,378
Two semitones make a bigger step:
a tone. Some call it a whole step.

45
00:03:02,978 --> 00:03:07,673
TAVI: So that's where the other
five come from: the gaps a tone wide.

46
00:03:08,023 --> 00:03:11,990
MERC: Yes. Five pairs of
neighbouring letters are a tone apart,

47
00:03:12,073 --> 00:03:14,552
and each gap holds one note.

48
00:03:14,902 --> 00:03:19,037
B to C and E to F have no gap to fill.

49
00:03:21,937 --> 00:03:26,059
Now the five need names.
They borrow them from their neighbours.

50
00:03:26,459 --> 00:03:32,809
Sharp means one semitone up.
Flat means one semitone down.

51
00:03:33,209 --> 00:03:36,609
[up from C to C sharp;
down from D to D flat]

52
00:03:36,959 --> 00:03:43,168
Up from C, it's C sharp. Down from D,
it's D flat. The same key.

53
00:03:43,518 --> 00:03:45,736
One key, two names.

54
00:03:46,336 --> 00:03:50,793
TAVI: So black keys are the sharps and
flats, and white keys are the plain ones?

55
00:03:51,193 --> 00:03:59,217
MERC: Not quite. Try it: one semitone
up from E. A black key, or a white one?

56
00:03:59,517 --> 00:04:01,517
[pause and predict]

57
00:04:01,817 --> 00:04:03,317
[E to F]

58
00:04:03,617 --> 00:04:12,308
White: F. So E sharp is another name
for F. Sharp is a direction, not a colour.

59
00:04:12,808 --> 00:04:15,182
TAVI: Then how do musicians choose a name?

60
00:04:15,482 --> 00:04:19,086
MERC: It depends on the music around it.
Both are right.

61
00:04:19,486 --> 00:04:25,227
When MercuryPitch draws your voice,
it uses the sharp names, like C sharp.

62
00:04:28,127 --> 00:04:30,654
TAVI: And how big is a semitone, exactly?

63
00:04:31,004 --> 00:04:36,928
MERC: Let's measure it. Listen to
a slow glide from A up to A sharp.

64
00:04:37,278 --> 00:04:40,878
[a slow glide from A3 up one semitone]

65
00:04:41,228 --> 00:04:42,667
That's 100 cents.

66
00:04:42,751 --> 00:04:48,662
In the tuning MercuryPitch uses,
every semitone is 100 cents wide.

67
00:04:49,162 --> 00:04:55,294
Twelve semitones make an octave,
so an octave is 1,200 cents.

68
00:04:55,794 --> 00:05:00,544
MercuryPitch tells you how far you
are from the nearest note, in cents.

69
00:05:00,894 --> 00:05:05,703
50 cents is halfway. Past that,
you're nearer the next note.

70
00:05:06,203 --> 00:05:11,517
Listen first. I'll hold an A,
slide up a semitone, and come back.

71
00:05:11,917 --> 00:05:16,117
[Merc sings A,
slides up a semitone and back]

72
00:05:16,417 --> 00:05:20,617
[Tavi slides up a semitone and back,
an octave higher]

73
00:05:20,967 --> 00:05:25,266
TAVI: How do I know I went up exactly
one semitone, and not a little more?

74
00:05:25,666 --> 00:05:31,134
MERC: Watch the monitor. A4 turns to A
sharp 4, with the marker in the middle.

75
00:05:31,217 --> 00:05:32,451
Then back again.

76
00:05:32,951 --> 00:05:37,478
Now you. Hum it quietly,
or sing it on ooh.

77
00:05:37,561 --> 00:05:41,235
Never push,
and stop if anything feels uncomfortable.

78
00:05:41,535 --> 00:05:47,382
Start on my A, or the A an octave up,
whichever feels easy.

79
00:05:47,465 --> 00:05:50,185
Slide up a semitone, and back.

80
00:05:50,585 --> 00:05:57,085
[your turn: slide up a semitone and back]

81
00:05:58,085 --> 00:05:58,918
Quick check.

82
00:05:59,202 --> 00:06:01,896
How many semitones make an octave?

83
00:06:02,196 --> 00:06:03,996
[think]

84
00:06:04,296 --> 00:06:08,459
Twelve. Seven letters,
and five notes in the gaps.

85
00:06:09,059 --> 00:06:12,946
Now listen to two steps.
Which one is the semitone?

86
00:06:13,296 --> 00:06:16,696
[two steps]

87
00:06:16,896 --> 00:06:18,696
[think]

88
00:06:18,996 --> 00:06:25,129
The second. B to C: two white keys,
but only one semitone.

89
00:06:25,729 --> 00:06:29,579
C sharp and D flat. One key, or two?

90
00:06:29,879 --> 00:06:31,679
[think]

91
00:06:31,979 --> 00:06:34,229
One key, two names.

92
00:06:34,729 --> 00:06:36,699
TAVI: And how many cents is a tone?

93
00:06:36,999 --> 00:06:38,799
[think]

94
00:06:39,099 --> 00:06:43,022
MERC: 200. Two semitones, 100 cents each.

95
00:06:43,522 --> 00:06:48,507
In Ear Lab's Leap,
listen for the semitone and the tone.

96
00:06:49,507 --> 00:06:50,480
Next time.

97
00:06:50,830 --> 00:06:56,600
Asked, most of 1,105 students
said they couldn't copy a tune.

98
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:03,299
Tested, only about one in ten to one
in six missed by a semitone or more.

99
00:07:03,799 --> 00:07:05,476
TAVI: So what's going on?

100
00:07:05,876 --> 00:07:08,793
[chime]
